   <dc:creator>
    <rdf:Seq>
     <rdf:li>Christopher L. Jerde and Mark L. Taper</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Seq>
   </dc:creator>
   <dc:title>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Assessing Models Instead of Testing Assumptions</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Assessing Models Instead of Testing Assumptions</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:title>
   <dc:subject>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>SIC; evidence functions; Welchâ€™s t-test; Studentâ€™s t-test; bootstrapping; statistical power; Behrens--Fisher</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:subject>
   <dc:type>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>Text</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:type>
   <dc:description>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Two-sample inference is commonly framed through Studentâ€™s or Welchâ€™s t-tests</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Two-sample inference is commonly framed through Studentâ€™s or Welchâ€™s t-tests</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">selected after testing assumptions of normality and equal variances. However</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">selected after testing assumptions of normality and equal variances. However</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">this workflow is fragile when assumptions are difficult to verify</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">this workflow is fragile when assumptions are difficult to verify</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and models are plausibly misspecified. Here</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and models are plausibly misspecified. Here</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">we evaluate the Behrens--Fisher problem through an evidential statistics framework that shifts inference from null hypothesis testing to direct comparison of competing models representing alternative data-generating processes. We formulate two-sample problems as generalized linear models that differ in location</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">we evaluate the Behrens--Fisher problem through an evidential statistics framework that shifts inference from null hypothesis testing to direct comparison of competing models representing alternative data-generating processes. We formulate two-sample problems as generalized linear models that differ in location</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">spread</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">spread</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and distribution</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and distribution</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and compare all models using likelihood-based evidence functions. We illustrate the approach with two contrasting datasets: halibut plasma cortisol concentrations with a small total sample size (n = 43) and cutthroat trout mass data from a long-term ecological study with a large sample size (n = 12</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and compare all models using likelihood-based evidence functions. We illustrate the approach with two contrasting datasets: halibut plasma cortisol concentrations with a small total sample size (n = 43) and cutthroat trout mass data from a long-term ecological study with a large sample size (n = 12</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">594). Model selection evidential comparisons show that the small halibut data provide insufficient information to discriminate among candidate models. Further bootstrap analyses reveal substantial model uncertainty and weak evidence for a difference in means. In contrast</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">594). Model selection evidential comparisons show that the small halibut data provide insufficient information to discriminate among candidate models. Further bootstrap analyses reveal substantial model uncertainty and weak evidence for a difference in means. In contrast</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">the cutthroat trout data decisively support a gamma-distributed model over normal alternatives</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">the cutthroat trout data decisively support a gamma-distributed model over normal alternatives</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">with strong</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">with strong</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">robust evidence for differences in the means. Empirical resampling for different sample sizes of the cutthroat data demonstrates that reliably detecting ecological effects can require sample sizes an order of magnitude or more beyond what is needed to resolve underlying statistical assumptions like normality and variance homogeneity; a gap with direct implications for statistical power</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">robust evidence for differences in the means. Empirical resampling for different sample sizes of the cutthroat data demonstrates that reliably detecting ecological effects can require sample sizes an order of magnitude or more beyond what is needed to resolve underlying statistical assumptions like normality and variance homogeneity; a gap with direct implications for statistical power</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">model selection practice</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">model selection practice</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and the replication crisis in ecology. These examples illustrate how evidential model comparison can assess the relative support for alternative model formulations</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and the replication crisis in ecology. These examples illustrate how evidential model comparison can assess the relative support for alternative model formulations</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">make model-selection uncertainty explicit</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">make model-selection uncertainty explicit</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and provide a practical framework for applied two-sample inference without relying on sequential assumption tests.</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and provide a practical framework for applied two-sample inference without relying on sequential assumption tests.</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:description>
